l rule. I am now trying to formally prove that the cone includes all cells whose `x` coordinate is defined by `|x - col_c| <= bottom_row - y`, where the range is CLIPPED to the grid's width and is formally definable.\n\n\n**Investigating Asymmetry**\n\nI'm now focusing on the off-center column. This raises questions about clipping versus symmetry. I'm exploring why the center isn't at column 4 in a 9-wide grid. I'm analyzing how the outputs behave when the center is off-center, particularly whether they are symmetric or clipped at the boundaries. The shape appears to be different based on the examples and boundaries. It is no longer just a simple cone!\n\n\n\n**Clarifying Expansion Rule**\n\nI realized my previous focus on edge behavior was unnecessary! The cone's expansion is naturally clipped by the grid boundaries. Rows with width less than grid width are simply due to the horizontal expansion hitting the edge. The key formula for expansion `|c - col_c| <= bottom_row - r` intrinsically handles the clipping. It's essentially a straightforward mathematical condition for grid rendering.\n\n\n",
